 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general municipal law, in relation to removing the\nrequirement that where a municipal corporation, school district or district\ncorporation issues indebtedness to finance the cost of preparation of plans\nand specifications for a proposed capital improvement, there must be a waiting\nperiod of at least one year before authorizing the undertaking of the capital\nimprovement",
    "basePrintNo": "S8525",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8525-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §99-d, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8525\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general municipal law, in relation to removing the\nrequirement that  where a municipal corporation, school district or\ndistrict corporation issues indebtedness to finance the cost of prepara-\ntion of plans and specifications for a proposed capital improvement,\nthere must be a waiting period of at least one year before authorizing\nthe undertaking of the capital improvement\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation would grant more flexibility to local governments as it\nwould allow them to undertake the financing of a capital project more\nquickly by eliminating the requirement that when the capital planning\ncosts are financed the project must wait at least one year before\nobtaining formal authorization to start.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Amends the general municipal law by eliminating subdivision 2\nof section 99-d, which requires a local authority to wait for a period\nof at least one year from the issuance of bonds or capital notes that\nwould be used to finance the preparation of preliminary plans and spec-\nifications for a capital improvement.\n\nSection 2. Sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn the course of developing a capital project which is expected to be\nfinanced by the issuance of bonds or notes, it is often necessary for a\nmunicipal corporation, school district or district corporation to engage\nengineers, architects or other professionals to prepare preliminary\nplans and specifications relating to the proposed project. Preliminary\nplans and specifications enable local officials to better understand the\nproject in terms of size, scope, appearance and other relevant aspects.\nSuch plans and specifications also provide an estimate of cost, which\npermits the municipality or school district to estimate the projected\ntax impact of the debt service associated with the project. In addition,\nthe environmental review required pursuant to the New York State Envi-\nronmental Quality Review Act (SEQRA) may be performed by the engineer or\narchitect engaged to assist in the early stages of planning the project.\n\nIn many cases the municipal corporation, school district or district\ncorporation does not have sufficient funds available to pay for the\npreliminary plans and specification and the Local Finance Law therefore\nprovides that, where appropriate, bonds or notes may be issued to\nfinance the preliminary costs. The authority to issue bonds or notes for\nthis purpose comes from Section 10.00 of the Local Finance Law, which\nprovides that a local government may contract indebtedness to finance\nany purpose for which a period of probable usefulness is provided under\nSection 11.00. Subdivision 62 of Section 11.00 of the Local Finance Law\nestablishes a period of probable usefulness of five years for the cost\nof preparation, pursuant to Section 99-d of the General Municipal Law,\nof surveys, preliminary plans, detailed plans, specifications and esti-\nmates for capital projects which may be undertaken in the future. A\nsignificant practical difficulty is presented by the special provision\nappearing in Section 99-d of the General Municipal Law, which provides\nthat if indebtedness is issued to finance the preparation of preliminary\nplans and specifications for a capital improvement, the municipal corpo-\nration, school district or district corporation must wait for a period\nof at least one year from the date of issuance of such indebtedness\nbefore authorizing the undertaking of the capital improvement. As a\nresult, neither a resolution authorizing the undertaking of the project,\nnor a bond resolution authorizing issuance of the construction bonds can\nbe legally adopted until one year after the initial planning bonds or\nnotes were issued. Where the construction bond resolution is subject to\na permissive referendum and an estoppel publication, the actual issuance\nof the construction bonds may have to be delayed by a period of 14\nmonths or more. This can impede the ability of the local government to\nconstruct t he project in a timely manner. Depending upon the actual\ntime of year, the required waiting period may cause the local government\nto miss an entire construction season, meaning that construction of the\nproject may be delayed by a period well in excess of 14 months.\n\nPursuant to Local Finance Law Section 11.00, preliminary costs associ-\nated with a particular capital improvement are deemed a part of the cost\nof the project and, therefore, may be included in the amount of bonds\nauthorized for the project. However, in many cases the municipal corpo-\nration, school district or district corporation has compelling reasons\nto authorize and issue indebtedness for preliminary costs separately\nfrom the construction costs. For example, the environmental review\nrequired under SEQRA may not yet be completed at the time when financing\nis needed to pay for preliminary costs. Pursuant to SEQRA, the bond\nresolution authorizing the construction bonds cannot be adopted until\nthe SEQRA review has been completed and, for that reason, it may be\nnecessary or desirable to issue a certain amount of bonds or notes to\nfinance preliminary costs separately from the construction bonds or\nnotes.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022: New legislation.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
